meta {
  name: Record Policy Transfer
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/crm/service-tickets/{{ticketId}}/policy-transfer
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "transferDate": "2026-03-10",
    "policies": [
      {
        "fromPolicyId": "{{fromPolicyId}}",
        "policyType": "Auto",
        "effectiveDate": "2026-03-15",
        "carrier": "Allstate",
        "policyNumber": "XFER-000123",
        "premium": 900,
        "itemCount": 1,
        "newBusinessApplication": {
          "key": "agencies/{{agencyId}}/policy-transfers/{{householdId}}/nba/app.pdf",
          "filename": "app.pdf",
          "contentType": "application/pdf",
          "size": 2048
        }
      }
    ],
    "submissionToken": "XFER-SESSION-0001"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns the refreshed ticket carrying the transfer", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.allowsPolicyTransfer).to.equal(true);
    expect(body.policyTransfer).to.not.equal(null);
    expect(body.policyTransfer.pairs).to.have.lengthOf(1);
  });

  test("links the retired policy to its replacement", function () {
    const pair = res.getBody().policyTransfer.pairs[0];
    expect(pair.fromPolicyId).to.be.a("string");
    expect(pair.toPolicyId).to.be.a("string");
    expect(pair.fromPolicyId).to.not.equal(pair.toPolicyId);
  });

  test("logs a system entry on the ticket", function () {
    const timeline = res.getBody().timeline;
    expect(timeline.some((e) => e.type === "system")).to.equal(true);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /crm/service-tickets/:id/policy-transfer

  Record a client's move from one package to another — a cheaper tier, a
  different product — **without booking new business**.

  - **Module:** `crm_service`
  - **Permission:** `crm_service:write`
  - **Data scope:** the **ticket is the clamp**. `getScopedOrThrow` 404s an
    out-of-scope ticket, and the household is then read off it, so nothing in
    the body can widen what a caller reaches. This replaces the Sold path's
    `loadOwnedLead`, which a leadless transfer has no equivalent of.

  ## What it is

  The **same intake pipeline as `POST /sold-deals`**, with a different anchor
  and a different label. A policy needs the same information to exist however it
  came about, so the policy rows, the documents, the discount block and the
  generated audit checklist are all identical. Three differences:

  | | Sold form | Policy transfer |
  |---|---|---|
  | Anchor | a lead | a **ticket** (and its household) |
  | `businessType` | `new_business` | **`company_transfer`** |
  | Prior insurance | asked | **never asked** — injected as `{ none: true }` |

  Prior insurance is omitted because the policy being replaced is already in our
  own book: there is no other carrier to name and nothing to cancel. Sending it
  is not just unnecessary, it is rejected — the fields are not in this schema.

  ## Allowed ticket categories

  `Renewal Review`, `Policy Change`, `Payment`, `Company Transfer`. Any other
  category is a `400`. Read `allowsPolicyTransfer` off the ticket rather than
  re-deriving the list client-side.

  ## Body

  | field | type | notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `transferDate` | `YYYY-MM-DD` | **The reporting date** — becomes `soldDateYmd`. Deliberately distinct from each policy's `effectiveDate`, so a policy that starts next month does not book its premium into a future period. |
  | `policies` | array, 1–10 | One row per policy being replaced. |
  | `policies[].fromPolicyId` | ObjectId | **Required.** The policy being retired. Must belong to the ticket's household — a cross-household pairing is a `400` and writes nothing. |
  | `policies[]` (rest) | — | Exactly `POST /sold-deals`'s policy row: `policyType`, `effectiveDate`, `carrier`, `policyNumber`, `premium`, `itemCount`, the required PDF `newBusinessApplication`, plus optional `discounts` / `escrow`. |
  | `submissionToken` | string | Per-session idempotency key, namespaced `XFER\|…` server-side. |

  ## What it writes

  1. A **`Deal`** with `businessType: 'company_transfer'`, `ticketId` set,
     `leadId` **null**, and `producerId` = the acting CSR.
  2. One **new `Policy`** per row, active, carrying `transferredFromPolicyId`.
  3. The **from-policy retired** — `active: false`, `policyStatus: 'Cancelled'`,
     `transferredToPolicyId` pointing forward. Both rows survive: the client
     genuinely held both over time.
  4. The **deal-audit checklist**, from the same discount triggers a sale uses.
  5. A `system` entry on the ticket timeline. **No `Activity` row** —
     `ACTIVITY_TYPES` has no "transferred" member, and a `sold` row for
     something that was not sold would be worse than none.
  6. `Household.totalActivePolicies`, recounted.

  ## Reporting

  This is the whole point. The premium lands on the **Transfers** scorecard
  (`GET /performance` → `transfers`) and is excluded from **Sold** and from the
  producer leaderboard. See `Performance/Get Performance (This Month).bru`.

  ## Idempotency

  **One transfer per ticket**, enforced by a partial-unique `{agencyId, ticketId}`
  index on `deals`. A second call is a `409`, not a second transfer.

  ## Response `201`

  The refreshed `ServiceTicketView`, with `policyTransfer` populated —
  `dealId`, `transferDate`, `premium`, `policyCount`, `premiumDelta` (negative
  is a saving; `null` if a from-policy no longer resolves), `recordedByName`,
  `dealAuditId`, and a `pairs[]` of from → to.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | Category not allowed; ticket has no household; from-policy on another household; carrier policy-number format; cross-branch discounts; duplicate policy number within the submission |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | `crm_service` disabled, or the caller lacks `crm_service:write` |
  | 404 | No such ticket, or out of scope; an uploaded document not found in storage |
  | 409 | A transfer already exists on this ticket |
}
